# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Secrets
.env
.env.local
*.pem
*.p8
credentials.json
token.json
service-account*.json
# The GCP TTS service-account key lands here. It is a private key —
# the filename (gcp-tts.json) matched none of the patterns above.
credentials/

# Audio artifacts — GENERATED ones. Briefing audio is written to out/ and
# tmp/ and must never be committed.
*.mp3
*.m4a
*.wav

# The music, which is the opposite case: three sets of intro/outro/
# interlude that ship WITH the app. The blanket *.mp3 above silently
# swallowed all nine on their first commit — `git add -A` reported the
# other files and said nothing about them, and the PR claimed they were
# committed when the repository held only a README describing them.
!assets/music/**/*.mp3

# And the waiting room's bed, which is the SAME case and was swallowed the
# same way — the exception above names `assets/music/` and this file lives
# in the iOS target's own Resources. Added 2026-09-13, after the first CI
# run in this project's history failed on its first attempt with
# `lstat(.../waiting-bed.mp3): No such file or directory`.
#
# It had been missing from the repository since task 4 merged. `WaitingRoom`
# lists it in the app target's resources build phase, so **the iOS app has
# not been buildable from a clean checkout since** — invisible to everyone,
# because every machine that had ever run the app had the file on disk.
#
# `WaitingRoomTests.testTheBedIsInTheBundle` exists precisely to stop this
# and could not: it asks the BUILT BUNDLE for the file, and locally the
# bundle has it. A test of the artefact cannot see a gap in the source.
!ios/Mercury/Resources/*.mp3
out/
tmp/

